{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c6d9-138d-7c82-92f1-7d0657d57a0f"},"sequence":77,"recorded_at":1780531400000149,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"69dcebcf-aab8-4829-be62-f7110c555cc6","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"69dcebcf-aab8-4829-be62-f7110c555cc6","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"69dcebcf-aab8-4829-be62-f7110c555cc6"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c6d9-8132-7153-8279-84cc65e280f9"},"task_id":"01a0c6d9-8132-7153-8279-84cc65e280f9","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c6d9-8132-7153-8279-84cc65e280f9","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev2.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer.md. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz, the SECOND of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i. Revision 1 was reviewed by RUN-260922-3770c1 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **five P1**, two P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev1.md in full first — every finding names a production file and line, a reviewer test that fails through the production entry, and a pinned SPEC line.\n3|What holds: all 8 operation entries are exercised through `Lifecycle.Execute`; B18 length checks exist and pass; the exec, probe and spawn adapters exist and the socket unlink crash test passes; the foreground fresh-decoy table is named and passes; tests ran with tmux unresolvable and pgrep empty, so the determinism constraint is met with zero real-tmux witnesses as declared. Keep all of that. **What is rejected is five production semantic defects, and the reviewer is right about each.** Do not argue them away with more mock assertions.\n4|Trunk has NOT moved. Build revision 2 on the rev1 candidate on top of the checkpoint `1ba06e9`. Do not refresh, do not rebase.\n5|\n6|1. **P1-A — safe-boundary success does not observe a safe boundary.** `exec.go:199` / `backend.go:observeBoundary` build the vector `wait-for -L ax-boundary-<generation>`. `-L` **acquires a channel lock**; on a fresh channel that succeeds immediately with no wrapper or provider signal whatsoever, and `observeBoundary` turns exit 0 into safe-boundary evidence. The requested provider proof kind is never passed into the observation. SPEC §4.C row 1208 and §4.E 1384 require a proof or a timeout. `TestReviewBoundaryNeedsSignal` reaches the production entry and fails on the vector; the committed `TestExecuteBoundary` scripts success for the wrong command. tmux(1) distinguishes lock acquisition from waiting for a signal. Separately, `lock-session` locks currently attached clients, whereas SPEC 1247/1383 requires rejecting **new** operator and provider input — that command alone implements no input barrier. Design and drive the actual barrier and boundary protocol, including new clients and provider inputs, and carry the requested proof kind into the observation.\n7|2. **P1-B — a read-only attach returns a writable execution vector.** `ops.go:executeAttach` and `exec.go:BuildCommand(DirectiveAttachSession)` omit the input authorization when building argv, so with `input_authorized=false` Execute returns exactly the same `attach-session -t ...` vector as for true. `TestReviewReadOnlyAttach` fails through Execute. Reporting `false` in `AttachOutcome` does not enforce anything on a caller that executes the vector. Preserve the authorization at the execution boundary and prove input cannot reach the pane. tmux documents `-r` for read-only attach; another enforced mechanism is fine, an informational boolean is not.\n8|3. **P1-C — probe failures manufacture absence and closure.** `backend.go:pollForAbsence`, `backend.go:terminateStale` and `status.go:probePanes` treat every nonzero exit as absence, and OSRunner converts a signal-killed child into `ExitCode=-1, nil` — so a timeout or a killed probe becomes successful closure. `TestReviewStopUnknownExit` drives `Execute(request-stop)` and gets a nil error with both `ProcessClosed` and `StoreClosed` true after a `-1` has-session result. SPEC status row 1206 says a read failure is **unknown, never absent**, and 1209-1210 require closure evidence for stop and stale termination. Distinguish positively observed absence from command, protocol, permission and cancellation failure; preserve the stderr and error classification you need to do it; and drive all three consumers. Narrowing to exit 1 alone is not sufficient unless the command failures that also exit 1 are distinguished too.\n9|4. **P1-D — custody follows an intermediate symlink.** `bind.go:checkCustodyAncestors` calls `EvalSymlinks` **before** its walk, so a symlinked intermediate component disappears from the path being checked; `TestReviewCustodyIntermediateSymlink` builds an alias above the valid root and `CheckSocketCustody` returns nil. That contradicts SPEC 809-812 component-by-component no-follow custody and the function's own no-symlink claim, and it means the B19/B20 closure this leaf claimed is incomplete. Use the existing descriptor-safe traversal owner — this repository already has the openat-relative walk, and lexical containment plus a resolved path is not containment. Do not paper over arbitrary symlinks by treating them as the macOS `/tmp` convention. Re-prove the before-connect and before-bind entries and identity stability afterwards.\n10|5. **P1-E — restore does not implement its required result contract.** `ops.go:executeRestore` returns only `PriorBindingID` and `RestoredParked`, leaving `OpOutcome.Binding` nil, while SPEC row 1176 requires `binding:TerminalInstanceBinding`. `TestReviewRestoreRequiredBinding` fails after a successful production Execute. \"No minter exists\" is not an external constraint that permits a different wire contract — compose the binding owner and return the required binding. And supply the after-restore ordered composition this leaf was assigned: the candidate only launches `ax pane`, unconditionally reports parked, and neither its restore witness nor its evidence drives the §4.2 1433-1441 sequence — local lease read, then a bounded refresh, then local resume / remote offer / park. Existing `axpane` decision tests do not show this new entry is wired to those effects. Preserve the ownership split, but supply the actual composition, the lapsed-local-grant remote-offer witness, and the **measured** refresh bound (a number, not the phrase \"without blocking forever\").\n11|6. **P2-A — replayed outcomes change their timestamps.** `lifecycle.go:executeEngineOp` regenerates `InputClosedAt` and `BoundaryObservedAt` from `Now` when a stored mutation result is replayed; `TestReviewQuiesceReplayTimestamp` advances the clock a second between identical requests and sees different closure timestamps with no second effect. SPEC 1207 binds evidence to closure time and generation and 1208 requires the same proof on retry. Persist and replay the complete operation-specific outcome including its original timestamps, and attack the crash and retry paths rather than only exec-count equality.\n12|7. **P2-B — the census misclassifies reachable cells, and the comparison is the forbidden one.** Your census defines `U2a` as \"entry never calls this code\" and then uses it for rootwrite and ancestorwrite at status and restore — but `Execute` calls `CheckSocketCustody` before dispatch, so those cells are reachable. Four independent entry-and-refusal-class narrowings (root/status, root/restore, ancestor/status, ancestor/restore) each leave the gate present, admit exactly one refusal class at one entry, and **SURVIVE the complete committed suite twice**; the reviewer's `TestReviewCustodyEntryRoots` passes on baseline and kills each one twice. Reclassify those cells as measured-or-bounded and ship the four missing refusal witnesses with their rows. The symmetry paragraph lists names but owes the required side-by-side row counts. The advertised 168/3648 is not an accepted measured ratio — restate it after the reclassification. Then the comparison: `outcome_compare.py` keys by **test name** and both JSON streams contain only `internal/tmuxserver`, which is exactly the single-package name-keyed shape the task forbids. The transitive importer closure of the nine named composition packages is 34 packages (the reviewer recorded it in `importer-closure.txt`); produce a real input/outcome comparison over it, and stop calling zero name-level regressions an outcome comparison. Finally, four AC witnesses you reference do not exist under those names (`TestExecuteRefusesNilDependencies` is actually `MissingDependencies`; `TestExecuteRestoreIdempotent`, `TestExecuteStatusPresentIncludesIdentity` and `TestObserveStatusAbsentProbe` are absent) — fix the names, and attach `TASK-260830-1c28dz_results.md` as a standalone board resource, because its bytes were readable only inside the tarball.\n13|8. **Read the reviewer's coverage section before you restate any ratio.** It measures 8 of 8 operation entries exercised and 33 of 33 acceptance rows having at least one passing named reference, and then says plainly that this is **test-execution coverage, not production conformance** — rows include helper-only witnesses, and the P1s above falsify specific claims (67/69, 79-83, 89). Do not carry 8/8 dispatch coverage forward as 8/8 correctly implemented effects. Report what you measure, with that distinction stated.\n14|\n15|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. The five P1s are production changes, so re-run the whole importer closure afterwards and name every input that moved across an arm. Run your own package tests, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` in the candidate config; another Story is producing concurrently, so command 5 will be slow — do not shorten it and report measured elapsed time and host load if a gate times out. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property: every rule driven to its effect with tmux unresolvable. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), the reclassified census with its measured ratio and the side-by-side symmetry counts, and the importer-closure comparison; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 2 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the five P1s cannot all be closed, hand off a reviewable slice and enumerate precisely what remains. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n16|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n17|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n18|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n19|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n20|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n21|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n22|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n23|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
